    {
      "id": "card.middle_ages.020.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Raiders, then rulers",
      "body": "Einhard wrote that Charlemagne built a fleet against the Northmen and posted guards at harbours and river mouths, because they kept raiding the coasts. The Vikings kept coming anyway. In England, Alfred beat them at Edington, then shared the land with the Danelaw. In France, the king gave Rollo Normandy. And William the Conqueror, Rollo’s great-great-great-grandson, took England in 1066 and then counted its pigs. The Vikings didn’t only raid Europe. Their descendants ended up running part of it.",
      "mascot": {
        "pose": "thumbs-up",
        "line": "From Lindisfarne to Domesday in ten levels. You sailed through that."
      }
    },
